event: content_block_delta
data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**Redis** is fast, gives you TTL-based expiry for abandoned carts for free, and fits the ephemeral nature of carts well. The downsides are that it's another stateful service to run if you don't already have one, and carts can be lost on eviction or restart unless you configure persistence. **Postgres** makes carts durable, survives restarts and eviction, and can be tied to a logged-in user across devices. It also makes abandoned-cart reporting a simple query. The costs are slightly higher latency per cart read and write, and cleanup of old carts becomes a job you have to schedule. Either way, the cookie should hold only an opaque cart ID, not the cart contents.\n\nI recommend **Postgres**, assuming you already run it, because carts are business data and losing them to a Redis eviction costs more than the extra latency. If Redis is already in your stack and carts are strictly anonymous and session-scoped, Redis is also a reasonable choice. I don't know your stack, so that's the part that could change the answer. The decision is yours, so I've recorded it as an ask."}}

