data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":2,"delta":{"type":"input_json_delta","partial_json":"{\"kind\": \"decision\", \"title\": \"Where should carts live: Redis or Postgres?\", \"body\": \"Carts currently sit in a 4 KB session cookie and long carts get truncated.\\n\\n**Option A: Redis.** Fast, built-in TTL for abandoned carts, fits anonymous session-scoped carts. Costs: another stateful service if not already running, and carts can be lost on eviction or restart without persistence configured.\\n\\n**Option B: Postgres.** Durable, survives restarts, can be tied to logged-in users across devices, and abandoned-cart reporting is a simple query. Costs: slightly higher latency per read/write, and you need a scheduled job to clean up old carts.\\n\\nEither way, the cookie should hold only an opaque cart ID.\\n\\n**Recommendation:** Postgres, if it's already in your stack. Choose Redis if it's already running and carts are strictly anonymous and session-scoped.\", \"actions\": [{\"kind\":\"resolve\",\"label\":\"Use Postgres (Recommended)\",\"recommended\":true},{\"kind\":\"resolve\",\"label\":\"Use Redis\"}]}"}}

